<BlogPostLayout title="Print Barcode Labels from CSV to PDF | BarcodeNest" heading="How to print barcode labels from a CSV spreadsheet" description="Turn a CSV of inventory SKUs or product barcodes into a printable PDF label sheet. Add titles, details and copies, preserve leading zeros and check print alignment." author="BarcodeNest Editorial Team" published="2026-11-24" readingTime="5 min read">
<div class="article-shell container"><aside class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#csv">Prepare the CSV</a><a href="#pdf">Create the PDF</a><a href="#printing">Print and check</a><a href="#limits">Understand limits</a></aside><div class="article-body">
<p>A barcode label workflow starts with a list of identifiers and the text you want beside each one. Export that list as CSV, choose a barcode type and produce a PDF sheet. This is useful for internal storage boxes, stockroom items and products whose assigned retail identifiers you already have.</p>
<p>The <a href="/barcode-labels/">BarcodeNest label printing tool</a> converts a CSV into an A4 PDF with two columns and five label positions per sheet. You can add a title, a detail line and a copy count. The fixed layout is intended for printing and alignment checks; it does not provide a custom template for every label-stock brand.</p>
<h2 id="csv">Prepare a barcode label CSV</h2><p>Create a column named <code>barcode</code>. Optional columns are <code>title</code>, <code>detail</code>, <code>copies</code> and <code>symbology</code>. This example creates three labels for one internal item and two for another:</p><CodeBlock label="Inventory label CSV" language="csv" code={csv} />
<p>Use <code>code128</code> for supported internal identifiers, <code>ean13</code> for complete EAN-13 numbers, <code>upca</code> for UPC-A, or <code>qr</code> for text you want encoded in a QR. If you omit the symbol type, the CSV workflow defaults to EAN-13. Set it explicitly when preparing internal SKU labels.</p>
<p>Keep barcode columns as text in your spreadsheet. Importing a UPC as a number can remove its first zero before you export it. Follow the <a href="/blog/keep-leading-zeros-barcodes-excel/">guide to preserving leading zeros in Excel</a>, and compare the exported CSV with your source identifiers. Put commas inside quoted CSV fields when a title or detail contains a comma.</p>
<h2 id="pdf">Upload the file and download the PDF</h2><ol><li>Create an account or sign in, then open the label printing tool.</li><li>Enter an active API key from your account and select your CSV file. The key stays in page memory and is sent to the API.</li><li>Check the identifiers, symbologies and copy counts in your source file. Use <strong>Check remaining units</strong> to confirm your label balance.</li><li>Select <strong>Create PDF labels</strong> and download the result for printing.</li></ol>
<p>Use short titles and details so they remain useful on the label. Printable Latin characters, common accented letters and currency symbols are supported. Long text is shortened, and a long Code 128 value can exceed the template's printable width. Reduce the content or choose another appropriate label workflow rather than forcing the barcode into a narrow space.</p>
<h2 id="printing">Print at actual size and check alignment</h2><p>Start with a plain-paper test. Select the correct paper size and actual-size printing, then compare the print against your intended label positions. Avoid “fit to page” unless you have deliberately accounted for its scaling. The browser CSV tool produces A4; the JSON label API also supports Letter paper.</p>
<p>Check all edges of the sheet, not just the first label. Printer feed and non-printable margins can affect alignment. Keep the barcode's clear margins intact, check contrast and avoid stretching individual labels to fit incompatible stock.</p>
<p>Scan a physical test print using your receiving system. You can also <a href="/blog/scan-barcode-from-image/">decode a photo of the printed barcode</a> to compare the encoded value with your source. A successful scan is a content check, not a formal print-quality grade. GS1's <a href="https://ref.gs1.org/guidelines/2d-in-retail">barcode quality guidance</a> explains why the scanning environment and final printed symbol matter.</p>
<h2 id="limits">Count copies when planning label usage</h2><p>Each printed label uses one label unit, including copies. The example above uses five units, not two. The tool supports up to 100 labels per request including copies. Free and One-time accounts include 25 labels per month, Creator 250, Starter 500 and Growth 2,500.</p>
<p>Label units are separate from generated image, scan and CSV enrichment units. Downloading an already generated PDF reuses that file; generating it again creates another operation. Check the remaining balance before resubmitting after an interrupted request. Website workflows and API keys share your account's allowances.</p>
<h2>Need product names before creating labels?</h2><p>If your spreadsheet contains assigned UPC or EAN identifiers but lacks titles, use <a href="/csv-product-enrichment/">CSV product enrichment</a> to retrieve available product details, then review those fields and prepare the label columns. Not every identifier has a catalog match. Internal SKUs generally require names from your own inventory system.</p>
<p>For one standalone image, use the <a href="/blog/create-barcode-svg-png/">SVG and PNG barcode generation guide</a>. For automated printing, see the <a href="/docs/barcode-tools/">PDF label API documentation</a>. Creating a label does not assign a retail GTIN or register a product.</p><p><a class="button button-signal" href="/barcode-labels/">Create your barcode label PDF</a></p>
